    {
      "id": "ledger-notes",
      "kind": {
        "text": "TWO ROUTES, AND NEITHER IS AN EXTERNAL SOLVER. This fact is settled twice: by enumerating every one of the 2^24 fp8 E5M2 triples in about three seconds, and by bit-blasting the guarded miter to CNF and refuting it in 25m46s with a re-checked DRAT proof. Alone in this batch, NEITHER settlement could be cross-checked against z3 or bitwuzla, and that is a measured fact about the field rather than an omission: z3 4.13.3 returns `unknown` on the file with `(:reason-unknown \"addition/subtract with ebits > sbits not supported\")`, because E5M2 is (_ FloatingPoint 5 3) and 5 > 3; bitwuzla 0.9.1 rejects the format outright as experimental, and the `--fpexp` escape named in its own error message is a BUILD option that the binary refuses as an invalid runtime flag. So axeyum decides, at this width, a claim that neither industrial FP solver can read -- which is the direct consequence of ADR-0023 making the FP builders generic over (exp_bits, sig_bits).\n\nWHY THE EXHAUSTIVE ROW CARRIES THE CHECKER AND THE SYMBOLIC ONE DOES NOT. 3s against 25m46s. The cheap row is also the one that rests on less: it does not go through axeyum's fpa2bv lowering, whose `add` is documented in its own source as a validated rather than proven bit-blaster, nor through Tseitin, nor through the SAT core. The footprint has to assume all three for the symbolic row, and none of them for the enumeration. Two routes agreeing across that gap is the evidence.\n\nTHE ARITY WALL. This claim is exhaustive at fp8 and nowhere else. 2^24 triples is seconds; the same claim at binary16 is 2^48 triples and at binary32 is 2^96. Unary claims escape it -- F:fp32-doubling-add-equals-mul-two is enumerated over all 2^32 binary32 values in under a minute -- so it is ARITY, not width, that decides whether brute force is available. The binary16 instance is recorded separately and is still `open`: see F:fp16-add-monotone-rne.\n\nSCOPE. E5M2 only. Nothing here transfers to E4M3, which has no infinities and an all-ones NaN, is therefore not an IEEE format, and which axeyum's `arithmetic_format_supported` gate refuses for exactly that reason.",
        "type": "prose"
      },
